---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Bug1 = `let score = 42;

if (score = 60) {
  console.log("Pass");
} else {
  console.log("Fail");
}`;
const Bug2 = `let score = 95;

if (score >= 60) {
  console.log("D");
} else if (score >= 70) {
  console.log("C");
} else if (score >= 80) {
  console.log("B");
} else if (score >= 90) {
  console.log("A");
} else {
  console.log("F");
}`;
const Bug3 = `let total = 50;

if (total < 50) {
  console.log("Shipping: $6.00");
} else {
  console.log("Free shipping");
}

if (total <= 50) {
  console.log("You just missed free shipping!");
}`;
const Bug4 = `let temp = 85;

if (temp > 80) {
  console.log("Hot");
}
if (temp > 60) {
  console.log("Warm");
}
if (temp <= 60) {
  console.log("Cold");
}`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Debug Challenge: Conditionals</h1>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <p>Four short programs, each with exactly one thing wrong. All four are about decisions &mdash; and every one of them runs perfectly and gives the wrong answer.</p>

          <p>
            <b>Three kinds of mistake show up in JavaScript.</b> A{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-red">syntax error</b> stops the program before it
            runs. A <b class="text-sn-orange">reference error</b> runs until it
            hits the bad name, so you get <i>partial output and then a
            crash</i> &mdash; that partial output is how you tell it from a
            syntax error. A <b class="text-sn-yellow">logic error</b> runs
            cleanly to the end and gives the wrong answer, which is the one
            that will cost you marks on an exam.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>All four of these are logic errors.</b> Not one of them
            produces an error message. If you find yourself waiting for one,
            that is exactly the habit this challenge exists to break.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="bugs">
          <h2>The Four Programs</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 1</h3>

          <p>This should say whether a score is a pass. It says <code>Pass</code> for every score.</p>

          <Code
            code={Bug1}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 1"
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 2</h3>

          <p>This should report a letter grade. A score of 95 prints <code>D</code>.</p>

          <Code
            code={Bug2}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 2"
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 3</h3>

          <p>This should charge shipping only on orders under $50. An order of exactly $50 is given free shipping &mdash; and then told it just missed it.</p>

          <Code
            code={Bug3}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 3"
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p><b>Also:</b> There are two conditions here and only one is wrong. Say which, and why the other is fine.</p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 4</h3>

          <p>This should print one message. It prints two.</p>

          <Code
            code={Bug4}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 4"
            class="mb-3"
          />
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>For each program, give:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>Which kind of mistake it is &mdash; syntax, reference, or logic</li>
            <li>One sentence on what was wrong</li>
            <li>The corrected line of code</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Work them out by reading first. Running the code tells you{" "}
            <i>that</i> something is wrong; it rarely tells you <i>why</i>, and
            on the exam there is no computer.
          </p>

          <p>Worth <b>3 points</b>, graded on completion.</p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Debugging
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
